          The 20, 35 and 50 pint Cube, with and without the pump, compared on certified capacity, how heavy the bucket gets, the four drain modes and what owners actually complain about.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#midea-cube-35-mad35s1qwt"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#top-picks"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Compare the Cube Models
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Shaheer Ali Khan</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Consumer Product Testing &amp; Materials Performance Specialist</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="14.6k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#how-we-chose" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. How We Researched It</a>
          <a href="#how-it-works" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. How the Cube Design Works</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. The 5 Models Compared</a>
          <a href="#which-size" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. 20 vs 35 vs 50 Pint</a>
          <a href="#drainage" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Bucket, Hose or Pump</a>
          <a href="#noise-app" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Noise, Controls and the App</a>
          <a href="#complaints" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. What Owners Complain About</a>
          <a href="#who-should" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Who Should and Shouldn't Buy</a>
          <a href="#vs-box" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Cube vs a Conventional Box</a>
          <a href="#care" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Keeping a Cube Running</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">12. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The Midea Cube is the dehumidifier people buy because it looks different, and the design is not a gimmick: a big bucket sits underneath, the machine sits on top, and the whole thing nests into a box for storage. The catch is that the bucket you were promised gets very heavy, the pump still needs you, and the 35 pint model is the one most homes should actually buy.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Researched the Cube Line
          </h2>

          <p>
            Midea sells the Cube in three capacities, 20, 35 and 50 pints a day, and the 50 pint comes with or without a built-in pump. Model numbers then multiply: there are white and gray finishes, original versions and newer revisions with an -A or -B suffix, and retailer-specific bundles. We started from the models Midea lists on its US site and in its current owner's manual, then kept only the versions we could match to a live Amazon listing by exact model number, so the product you click is the product we describe.
          </p>

          <p>
            For every number on this page we used the most authoritative source we could find. Capacity and annual energy use come from the ENERGY STAR certified product database, which publishes the tested water removal under the current Department of Energy procedure. Bucket fill levels, the pump's lift height, the operating temperature range and the error codes come from Midea's 2024 Cube user manual, which covers the MAD20S1QWT, MAD35S1QWT, MAD50S1QWT and MAD50PS1QWT. Weights and dimensions come from Midea's own product pages.
          </p>

          <p>
            Noise is where published figures disagree, so we name the source each time. Midea does not print a decibel rating in the manual, a Home Depot listing gives one figure, and independent reviewers measured higher ones. Owner complaints come from retailer review pages and review sites, and we describe them as patterns rather than verdicts. We did not test these units ourselves for this page.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you have not decided on Midea yet, start with our <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-home/">best dehumidifier for home</a> guide, which compares the Cube 35 against Frigidaire, Honeywell and hOmeLabs. This page is for people who already like the Cube and want to know which one to order, whether the pump is worth paying for, and what to expect after the first month.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-it-works" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How the Cube Design Works
          </h2>

          <p>
            A normal dehumidifier is a tall box with a small bucket that slides out of the front or the bottom. The Cube flips that around. The dehumidifier itself is a squat block that sits on top of a separate square bucket, and the bucket is most of the machine's footprint. Midea markets this as a lift-and-twist design: you lift the unit off, rotate it 90 degrees, and lower it into the bucket to nest it for storage or shipping. To run it, you rotate it back so the arrows on the unit and bucket line up and set it on top.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical payoff is the bucket size. Midea's manual lists four fill levels you choose with a button. On the 20 pint model they run from about 1.5 gallons to 3 gallons. On the 35 and 50 pint models they run from about 2.25 gallons to 4.25 gallons. A typical tower dehumidifier bucket holds well under 2 gallons, which is where Midea's claim of up to three times the water capacity comes from. More water per trip means fewer trips.
          </p>

          <p>
            The less advertised consequence is weight. The same manual gives the weight of a full bucket at each fill level, and at level 4 the 35 and 50 pint bucket weighs about 40.5 pounds. That is a bucket of water you have to carry to a sink and tip from a front corner, because pouring from the back can spill through the relief holes. The fill level button exists for exactly this reason: set it to level 1 or 2 and the unit stops at about 24 or 30 pounds of water instead.
          </p>

          <p>
            Midea's manual describes four ways to run the Cube: bucket mode, bucketless mode, continuous draining with a hose and, on the pump model, drain pump mode. Each has its own setup steps, and two of them have quirks worth knowing before you buy, which we cover in the drainage section below. Casters clip into the bottom corners of the bucket, but the manual says not to roll the bucket with water in it, and to lift the unit off first.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE SHORT VERSION</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Buy the Cube for the bucket and the storage, not the looks.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If you will drain by hose all season, the Cube's big bucket stops mattering and a conventional box does the same work. If you empty by hand or put the unit away every winter, the Cube's design genuinely earns its price.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The 5 Midea Models Worth Comparing
          </h2>

          <p>
            Four of these are the Cube in its current US forms, and the fifth is Midea's own conventional 50 pint box, included because it is the honest alternative if you do not need the Cube's tricks. Capacities and energy figures come from the ENERGY STAR product database, operating details from Midea's Cube owner's manual, and sizes and weights from Midea's product pages. We researched these from published specifications and named reviews; we did not run our own tests on these units for this page. The links below are affiliate links, which do not change the order or what we say about each model's weaknesses.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <div id={product.id} class="scroll-mt-24 mt-12 first:mt-8">
