        <div id="which-size" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. 20 vs 35 vs 50 Pint: Which Size
          </h2>

          <p>
            The pint rating is how much water the unit removes in 24 hours under the DOE test, which since 2019 has been run at 65 degrees Fahrenheit and 60 percent humidity. That is a cool, damp basement condition, so the numbers are realistic for a lower level but they will be beaten on a hot, sticky day upstairs. ENERGY STAR lists the Cube 20 at 20.16 pints a day, the current 35 pint revision at 35.82, and the 50 pint models at 49.67.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Model</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Certified pints/day</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Annual energy</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Max bucket</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Midea coverage</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cube 20 (MAD20S1QWT)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20.16</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">249 kWh</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3 gal</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,500 sq ft</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cube 35 (MAD35S1QWT)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">35.82*</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">378 kWh*</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4.25 gal</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3,500 sq ft</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cube 50 (MAD50S1QWT-A)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">49.67</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">515 kWh</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4.25 gal</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4,500 sq ft</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cube 50 Pump (MAD50PS1QWT)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">49.67*</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">515 kWh*</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4.25 gal</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4,500 sq ft</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The asterisks mark figures ENERGY STAR lists for the -A revision of that model; the Amazon listings for the 35 and the pump model carry the original model number without the suffix. The efficiency figure also separates the sizes. The Cube 20 is rated at 1.70 liters per kilowatt-hour, while the 35 and 50 are both rated at 2.01. In plain terms, the 35 and 50 remove more water for each unit of electricity, so a larger Cube running fewer hours is not a waste of power.
          </p>

          <p>
            Square footage claims are the least useful number on the box. Midea rates the Cube 20 for 1,500 square feet, the 35 for 3,500 and the 50 for 4,500, but how damp the space is matters far more than how big it is. A finished 800 square foot basement with a wet foundation can need a 50, while a large, dry main floor may be fine with a 20. Our <a href="/what-size-dehumidifier-for-basement/">basement sizing guide</a> walks through the dampness classes properly.
          </p>

          <p>
            For most people the 35 is the right Cube. It uses the same large bucket as the 50, weighs about two pounds less at 39.62 pounds according to Midea, and its certified capacity is a sensible middle for a typical damp basement or a muggy lower level. The 20 makes sense for a single bedroom, office or small apartment, and we cover it alongside other compact units in our <a href="/best-small-dehumidifier/">small dehumidifier guide</a>. The 50 is for genuinely wet spaces, laundry drying and open basements where the 35 would run all day. If you are weighing it against other brands at that size, see our <a href="/best-50-pint-dehumidifier/">best 50 pint dehumidifier</a> comparison.
          </p>

          <p>
            Energy cost follows capacity. At 515 kWh a year, the 50 pint Cube's ENERGY STAR figure is about double the Cube 20's 249 kWh. That figure assumes a standard number of operating hours, so your bill depends on how damp the space is and how low you set the target. Our guide to <a href="/how-much-electricity-does-a-dehumidifier-use/">how much electricity a dehumidifier uses</a> shows how to turn those numbers into dollars at your utility's rate.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="drainage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Bucket, Bucketless, Hose or Pump
          </h2>

          <p>
            Bucket mode is the default. The unit sits on the bucket, runs until the water reaches the fill level you set, then shows P2 on the display and stops. In a damp basement in midsummer, a 50 pint Cube removing close to its rating would fill a 4.25 gallon bucket in well under a day, which is why most owners end up using a hose or the pump within the first week.
          </p>

          <p>
            Continuous draining uses the hose port on the back of the bucket. You remove a plastic cover, insert the included hose or screw on an ordinary garden hose, and run it to a floor drain, sump or utility sink that sits lower than the port. Gravity does the work, so the hose has to slope downhill the entire way. Keep the cover: if you go back to bucket mode without it, the water simply runs out of the back onto the floor, which is the cause the manual lists first under water on the floor.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bucketless mode is the odd one. It lets you run the unit without its bucket, sitting directly on a surface with a hose attached to the drain port on the dehumidifier itself. The manual says it must be enabled within three minutes of plugging the unit in, by holding the up and fill level buttons together, and it is the one setting that does not survive a power cut: auto restart brings back every previous setting except bucketless mode. If you live somewhere with frequent outages, use the bucket with a hose instead.
          </p>

          <p>
            The pump model adds a small pump that sits inside the bucket and pushes water up and out through a separate hose. Midea's manual says it can pump up to 14 feet above the drain port, which is enough to reach a utility sink, a basement window or a laundry standpipe from the floor. Two details matter. The pump does not start until the water reaches fill level 4, and it deliberately leaves some water in the bucket so it never runs dry, which is why the manual tells you to empty the bucket at least once a week even in pump mode. If you route the hose outdoors, the manual says not to run it at or below 32 degrees, because the hose will freeze and the unit will stop.
          </p>

          <p>
            Is the pump worth it? Only if you have no floor drain or sink below the unit. If gravity can do the job, the plain Cube 50 with a hose is simpler and has one fewer part to fail. If the only drain is upstairs or out a window, the pump is the difference between a dehumidifier you use and one you resent. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-drain-a-dehumidifier-without-a-floor-drain/">draining a dehumidifier without a floor drain</a> covers the alternatives, and <a href="/best-dehumidifier-with-pump/">our pump dehumidifier roundup</a> compares the Cube against other pump models.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="noise-app" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Noise, Controls and the SmartHome App
          </h2>

          <p>
            Midea's manual does not publish a noise rating for the Cube, and the figures that circulate disagree. A Home Depot listing for the Cube 20 gives 45 dB. Tom's Guide, reviewing the same 20 pint model, reported it ran at around 55 decibels and found a metallic hum somewhat annoying. HouseFresh measured 54.2 dBA from three feet on top speed on the Cube it reviewed. The gap is mostly method: a low-speed figure from the maker against a high-speed measurement at close range.
          </p>

          <p>
            For planning purposes, assume the Cube sounds like any other compressor dehumidifier on high, which is roughly the level of a loud conversation, and noticeably quieter on low. It has only two fan speeds, high and low, which is one fewer than many tower units. That is fine in a basement but limits how quietly you can run it in a bedroom. If quiet is the priority, our <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-bedroom/">bedroom dehumidifier guide</a> compares maker-rated noise across brands.
          </p>

          <p>
            The control panel is straightforward. The mode button cycles between Set, which runs to a humidity target you adjust in 5 percent steps, Max, which runs continuously on high fan, and Continuous, which runs nonstop at the fan speed you choose. A timer turns the unit on or off after up to 24 hours, the filter light reminds you to wash the filter, and auto defrost cycles the compressor off when frost forms on the coils. The manual rates the unit for rooms between 41 and 95 degrees Fahrenheit.
          </p>

          <p>
            Every Cube has Wi-Fi and works with Midea's SmartHome app on iOS and Android, which shows room humidity, changes the target and fan speed and switches the unit on and off. Midea also advertises voice assistant compatibility. The wireless module listed in the manual runs on the 2.4 GHz band only, and HouseFresh noted that the app would not connect over 5 GHz Wi-Fi. If your router combines both bands under one name, expect to fiddle with setup. The app is genuinely useful for a unit that lives in a basement you rarely visit, and worth nothing if you set the target once and forget it. Our <a href="/best-smart-dehumidifier/">best smart dehumidifier</a> guide compares the Cube's app with other brands'.
          </p>

          <p>
            Learn the error codes before you need them. P2 means the bucket has reached its fill level. Eb means the unit has been lifted off the bucket or is not seated correctly. AS and ES are sensor errors, E3 is a general malfunction, and EC means the unit has detected a refrigerant leak. The manual's first fix for the sensor and malfunction codes is to unplug the unit and plug it back in, and to call Midea if the code returns.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="complaints" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. What Owners Complain About
          </h2>

          <p>
            The most consistent complaint is the one the design invites: a full bucket is heavy and awkward to empty. A HouseFresh reviewer said the 4.2 gallon bucket gets really heavy when full and that some people will struggle to empty it, and a Sam's Club reviewer returned a 50 pint unit because emptying it was too cumbersome. The manual's own weights back this up. Lowering the fill level, or using a hose or pump, is the fix.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second pattern is early failure. Several reviewers on Lowe's describe Cubes that stopped working after a year or two, and one Home Depot reviewer said a unit quit at about 12.5 months before Midea sent a replacement. These are individual reports, not failure rates, and every dehumidifier brand collects them, but they matter because the warranty is one year. HouseFresh pointed out that this is short against the five years it cited for Honeywell.
          </p>

          <p>
            Third, the pump. Summaries of Amazon reviews mention pump problems and noise, and the manual itself warns that the pump may be loud for the first three to five minutes of operation. When the pump light blinks, the manual's checklist is to clean the pump filter, check the hose for blockages, empty the bucket and make sure the bucket and hose are seated properly. Owners who skip the weekly bucket check are more likely to see the pump fault.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fourth, the app. Setup trouble on dual-band routers comes up repeatedly, and one owner reported the app not registering the bucket's water level. A single owner of a Cube 20 claimed the humidity reading was far off, which is worth checking against a cheap separate hygrometer on any dehumidifier, not just this one.
          </p>

          <p>
            On the other side of the ledger, the same review pages commonly praise how fast the Cube pulls down humidity, the bucket capacity when it is not full, and how neatly it stores. HouseFresh's reviewer said they had used the gravity hose for over three years with no issues. The fair summary is that the Cube's problems are mostly about handling and a short warranty, not about drying performance.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Who Should Buy a Cube, and Who Shouldn't
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buy a Cube if you empty the bucket by hand and want to do it less often. The large bucket genuinely cuts trips, and the fill level button lets you trade capacity for a weight you can manage. It is also the best design we know of for anyone who puts a dehumidifier away for the winter: nested in its bucket, the Cube 35 and 50 take up about the same space as a large storage tote, around 14.5 inches on each side according to Midea's dimensions.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy one if you will use the app. A dehumidifier in a basement, crawl space access room or vacation home is out of sight, and being able to see the humidity and change the target from your phone is useful there. Buy the pump model if your only drain is above floor level and you want hands-off running.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not buy a Cube if you will run it on a hose all season in a basement with a floor drain. In that case the bucket you are paying for never fills, and a conventional dehumidifier, including Midea's own box model or the GE units in our <a href="/ge-dehumidifier-review/">GE dehumidifier review</a>, does the same drying for the same ENERGY STAR efficiency at 50 pints. Do not buy one for a room that drops below 41 degrees; the manual's range stops there, and our <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-garage/">garage dehumidifier guide</a> covers units suited to cold spaces.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip it, too, if lifting is a problem in your household. Even at the lowest fill level a 35 or 50 pint Cube's bucket holds about 2.25 gallons and weighs about 24 pounds, and the unit itself weighs about 40 pounds when you lift it off. If nobody in the house can do that comfortably, plan on the hose or pump from day one, or choose a machine with a smaller front-loading bucket.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, be cautious about the bathroom. Midea's manual says not to operate the unit in an excessively humid room such as a bathroom or laundry room, which is at odds with some retailer listings that market it for bathrooms. A Cube in a hallway or adjacent room with the door open is fine; inside a steamy shower room is not what the manual has in mind. Our <a href="/best-bathroom-dehumidifier/">bathroom dehumidifier guide</a> covers that job separately.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-box" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Cube vs a Conventional Box Dehumidifier
          </h2>

          <p>
            Midea makes a direct comparison easy, because it sells a conventional 50 pint dehumidifier, the MAD50C1AWS-A, alongside the Cube. ENERGY STAR lists it at 49.74 pints a day, an efficiency of 2.01 liters per kilowatt-hour and 525 kWh a year, which is effectively the same drying and running cost as the 50 pint Cube's 49.67 pints, 2.01 and 515 kWh. Inside, they are doing the same job; the difference is the shell around them.
          </p>

          <p>
            What the Cube gives you over the box is a much bigger bucket, a compact nested shape for storage and the fill level control. What the box gives you is a familiar upright shape with a smaller bucket that is lighter to carry when full. Retailer listings put the box model at about 24 inches tall and roughly 40 pounds, so it is not lighter as a machine, only as a bucket.
          </p>

          <p>
            The same logic applies against other brands. GE's 50 pint ADHE models have a 15 pint bucket and 3 fan speeds, Frigidaire and Honeywell sell similar towers, and all of them carry ENERGY STAR ratings in the same band. Our <a href="/ge-dehumidifier-review/">GE dehumidifier review</a> compares that lineup, and our <a href="/best-50-pint-dehumidifier/">best 50 pint dehumidifier</a> guide puts the leading models side by side. None of them dries meaningfully faster than the Cube; you are choosing a bucket, a shape and a warranty.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our view: if your plan is a hose to a floor drain, buy whichever 50 pint ENERGY STAR model is cheapest that week, and the Midea box is a good candidate. If you will empty by hand, store it seasonally or move it between rooms, the Cube is worth choosing, and the 35 is the sweet spot.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="care" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Keeping a Cube Running
          </h2>

          <p>
            The air filter is a washable mesh on the sides of the unit. Pull it outward, rinse it in clean water, let it dry and put it back, then hold the fan button for three seconds to clear the filter light. A clogged filter starves the coils of air, which makes the unit run longer and frost more often. In a dusty unfinished basement, check it every couple of weeks during the humid season.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clean the bucket every few weeks. The manual says to swish a little mild detergent and water around, empty and rinse, and specifically not to put the bucket in a dishwasher. Standing water in a dark basement grows mildew and bacteria, and a slimy bucket is the most common source of the musty smell people blame on the dehumidifier itself. On the pump model, also rinse the pump's mesh filter, which collects the debris that would otherwise jam the pump.
          </p>

          <p>
            Give the unit space. The manual asks for at least 8 inches of clearance on all sides and at least 16 inches above the air outlet, and it should sit level on a floor strong enough to hold a full bucket. A unit pushed against a wall or tilted on an uneven slab can be noisy, which the troubleshooting table lists as the first cause of a loud unit. When you first set it up, the manual recommends running it continuously for 24 hours.
          </p>

          <p>
            At the end of the season, let it sit unplugged for a full day so the coils dry, clean the filter and bucket, tuck the cord into its channel, and nest the unit in the empty bucket. The manual warns twice to make sure the bucket is empty before nesting. Carry the nested unit by the bucket handle, not the unit.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep your receipt. The Cube's warranty is one year of full coverage from the purchase date, serviced through Midea Consumer Services at 1-866-646-4332, and the manual excludes commercial and rental use. Given the early-failure reports, register the unit and note the purchase date, because a claim at month eleven is a replacement and a failure at month thirteen is a new dehumidifier.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the 50 because bigger sounds safer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The 35 and 50 share the same bucket and efficiency, and the 50 uses about 515 kWh a year against the 35's 378 in ENERGY STAR's figures. Unless your space is genuinely wet or the 35 would run constantly, the extra capacity mostly adds running cost and fills the bucket faster.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving the fill level at 4 and then struggling to carry it</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                At level 4 the 35 and 50 pint bucket holds about 4.25 gallons and weighs about 40.5 pounds, per Midea's manual. If that is too heavy, set level 1 or 2 so it stops at about 24 or 30 pounds. You empty more often, but you can actually carry it to the sink.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Removing the drain cover and forgetting it</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Continuous draining starts by removing a plastic cover on the back of the bucket. If you later switch back to bucket mode without replacing it, water runs straight onto the floor. The manual lists this as a cause of water on the floor. Keep the cover somewhere you will find it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting the pump to empty the bucket completely</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Midea's pump does not start until the water reaches fill level 4 and stops with some water left so it never runs dry. That is by design. The manual still asks you to empty the bucket at least weekly, and skipping that is a common route to pump faults and musty water.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using bucketless mode where the power goes out</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Bucketless mode has to be enabled within three minutes of plugging in, and it is the one setting auto restart does not restore after a power cut. After an outage the unit expects its bucket. If outages are common, run it on the bucket with a hose instead.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which Midea Cube size should I buy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most basements and living areas, the 35 pint. It shares the 50's 4.25 gallon bucket and 2.01 L/kWh efficiency but uses less power, at 378 kWh a year against 515 in ENERGY STAR's figures. Choose the 20 for a single room and the 50 for genuinely wet spaces or indoor laundry drying.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much water does the Midea Cube bucket hold?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Midea's manual lists four fill levels. The Cube 20 holds about 1.5, 2, 2.75 or 3 gallons. The 35 and 50 pint models hold about 2.25, 3, 3.75 or 4.25 gallons, which weighs about 40.5 pounds at the top level. You choose the level with the fill level button to keep the bucket liftable.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How high can the Midea Cube pump push water?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Midea's owner's manual for the MAD50PS1QWT says the pump can lift water up to 14 feet above the drain port. That reaches most utility sinks, laundry standpipes and basement windows. The pump starts when the bucket reaches fill level 4, and the manual says to empty the bucket weekly even in pump mode.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the Midea Cube loud?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Midea's manual does not publish a noise rating. A Home Depot listing gives 45 dB for the Cube 20, Tom's Guide reported around 55 decibels for the same model, and HouseFresh measured 54.2 dBA at three feet on high. Expect typical compressor noise on high and noticeably less on low; it has only two fan speeds.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does the Midea Cube work in a cold basement or garage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only down to 41 degrees Fahrenheit. Midea's manual rates it for rooms between 41 and 95 degrees, and says coils can frost at lower temperatures, which reduces performance. Auto defrost helps near the bottom of that range, but for an unheated garage or crawl space in winter, look at units built for cold operation.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What app does the Midea Cube use?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Midea's SmartHome app on iOS and Android. It shows room humidity and lets you change the target, fan speed and power from your phone, and Midea advertises voice assistant support. The wireless module runs on 2.4 GHz only, so if your router merges 2.4 and 5 GHz under one name, setup can take some fiddling.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What warranty does the Midea Cube have?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                One year of full coverage from the original purchase date, according to the warranty in Midea's Cube manual, with service through Midea Consumer Services at 1-866-646-4332. That is shorter than some rivals, and because owners report some failures around the one to two year mark, keep the receipt and register the unit.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
